WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.250
Some clients aren't E R P systems. They're platforms with their own A P I, like a marketplace we'll call BuyHub.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:16.475
BuyHub buys by E D I, but receives confirmations and invoices as Jason through its A P I. We translate each one exactly.

00:00:16.475 --> 00:00:22.425
Think of an interpreter: every word lands in the other language, same meaning, nothing extra.

00:00:22.425 --> 00:00:30.400
Start with the eight fifty five, the order confirmation. Each line has an ack code: fulfilled, rejected, or left open.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:39.325
I A, I P, and I S mean accepted. Backordered, I B, is accepted too; the later date travels as the expected delivery.

00:00:39.325 --> 00:00:47.800
I R, and R one to R seven, mean rejected, with the reason in the line error. I H means on hold: the line stays open.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:57.725
The gotcha: BuyHub accepts exactly one confirmation per P O. A second post gets a four oh nine conflict. So check first, and post once.

00:00:57.725 --> 00:01:08.475
Buyers also want the supplier's order number back. One supplier sends it in B A K zero eight, another in R E F C O. Our mapper picks the source per sender.

00:01:08.475 --> 00:01:12.450
Now the eight ten, the invoice. Money is where bugs hide.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:22.325
Totals and charges are type N two, with two implied decimals. So T D S fifteen sixty nine means fifteen dollars and sixty nine cents.

00:01:22.325 --> 00:01:31.200
Tax can sit at the header or on the lines: don't count it twice. Each charge code goes to its own field, like shipping or fuel surcharge.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:40.050
Then always re-add the lines, plus tax, plus charges, and compare with the total. If they disagree, stop. Never post a wrong invoice.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:51.300
Some invoices need a decision. A credit memo has negative quantities. Posted as a normal invoice, it's a negative invoice. Ask the buyer how credits should arrive.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.200
An invoice with no real P O can't match any order. Hold it and ask.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:04.900
Two more traps. First, a two oh one success is not proof. An A P I can say success and still drop a field it doesn't store.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:09.275
So when a new field goes live, ask the buyer to confirm they see it.

00:02:09.275 --> 00:02:16.825
Second, duplicates. One extra click can post twice. After a batch, check for exactly one job per document.

00:02:16.825 --> 00:02:21.450
If a duplicate slipped through, tell the buyer right away which one to ignore.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:31.525
To recap: map codes exactly, post once, treat N two as implied decimals, re-add totals, hold odd invoices, and verify on the buyer's side.

00:02:31.525 --> 00:02:35.225
Next, we cross the ocean, to Europe and edi-fact.

